1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,900
Imagine, um, just for a moment, that you are standing in this big, empty field.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,040
Okay, setting the scene.

3
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:16,220
Yeah, exactly. The wind is blowing, the grass is high, and you have just been given the most daunting, like, truly terrifying assignment in human history.

4
00:00:16,400 --> 00:00:16,960
Which is?

5
00:00:17,100 --> 00:00:20,880
You are tasked with rebuilding society from absolute scratch.

6
00:00:21,080 --> 00:00:23,440
Oh, wow. That is a lot of pressure.

7
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:30,360
Right. Every institution, every law, every government building, every currency you've ever known has completely vanished.

8
00:00:31,120 --> 00:00:32,580
You have a totally blank slate.

9
00:00:32,720 --> 00:00:33,760
Just you in the grass.

10
00:00:34,060 --> 00:00:36,140
Yeah, and millions of people are looking at you waiting.

11
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:41,200
So what is the very first thing your new government absolutely must do?

12
00:00:41,440 --> 00:00:47,140
I mean, most people would probably sit down by a campfire and, I don't know, try to draft a constitution.

13
00:00:47,640 --> 00:00:51,300
Right, or maybe you start passing laws to keep people from stealing from each other.

14
00:00:51,300 --> 00:00:53,600
Or you figure out a way to collect taxes.

15
00:00:53,920 --> 00:00:55,900
Or maybe you just crown a leader, you know?

16
00:00:56,180 --> 00:01:01,080
Find the smartest or the strongest person and say, uh, okay, you're in charge, tell us what to do.

17
00:01:01,600 --> 00:01:06,500
Exactly. Because most of us, when we think about government, we immediately jump to the language of authority.

18
00:01:06,880 --> 00:01:10,580
Yeah, we think about who has the power to tell other people what to do.

19
00:01:11,100 --> 00:01:13,000
It's completely natural that we go there.

20
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:17,020
I mean, our entire historical vocabulary for politics is built around this.

21
00:01:17,020 --> 00:01:29,720
Oh, absolutely. If you trace it all the way from Plato in ancient Greece, through Thomas Hobbes and John Locke, right up to modern political science, it's all built around this central pillar of sovereignty.

22
00:01:30,120 --> 00:01:35,580
We are just obsessed with the idea of legitimacy as this form of, like, rightful domination.

23
00:01:35,940 --> 00:01:38,140
We constantly ask who ought to rule.

24
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:43,060
And by what moral or historical right do they hold that power over the rest of us?

25
00:01:43,180 --> 00:01:46,440
We treat government like it's a throne that someone gets to sit on.

26
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:49,500
But today's deep dive is going to completely dismantle that throne.

27
00:01:49,960 --> 00:01:54,320
We are looking at a genuinely mind-bending monograph from 2026.

28
00:01:54,520 --> 00:01:56,400
Right. The one by the researcher known as Flyxion.

29
00:01:56,600 --> 00:02:00,780
Yes. It's called Constitutional Cybernetics, a control theoretic theory of government.

30
00:02:01,220 --> 00:02:09,040
And the mission for this deep dive is to completely rewire how you think about politics, human rights, and even democracy itself.

31
00:02:09,040 --> 00:02:13,880
We are basically taking that traditional language of authority and legitimacy and tossing it out the window.

32
00:02:14,400 --> 00:02:15,180
Right out the window.

33
00:02:15,180 --> 00:02:20,500
In its place, we are bringing in the language of cybernetics and control theory.

34
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:24,180
Which sounds super sci-fi, but it's incredibly grounded.

35
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:25,760
Okay, let's unpack this.

36
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:33,200
The core premise we are starting with is that a government is not a static institution that rules over you.

37
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:33,600
Right.

38
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:39,860
It is a dynamic, continuously operating feedback loop of estimation and control.

39
00:02:39,860 --> 00:02:45,340
Yeah. Flickrise is really demanding a radical fundamental shift in our perspective here.

40
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:46,100
How so?

41
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:55,800
Well, if we genuinely want to understand what a government is and why it fails or succeeds, we have to stop looking at static snapshots of institutions.

42
00:02:56,060 --> 00:02:57,840
Like organizational charts and that sort of thing.

43
00:02:57,840 --> 00:02:59,560
Exactly. We have to stop looking at those.

44
00:02:59,560 --> 00:03:06,400
We are shifting entirely away from asking who ought to rule to asking what any governing system must actually accomplish in real time.

45
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:09,080
Basically, how to keep society from crashing into a wall.

46
00:03:09,320 --> 00:03:12,820
You got it. It frames governance purely as an engineering problem.

47
00:03:12,880 --> 00:03:14,860
So it's not about morality or divine right.

48
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:16,880
No, it's about survival.

49
00:03:17,880 --> 00:03:28,960
If a society is going to survive the next century, it has to continually observe itself, figure out what state it's currently in, compare that to where it wants to be, and then take corrective action.

50
00:03:29,160 --> 00:03:32,980
So it's not about issuing grand moral commands from on high.

51
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:40,860
Exactly. It's about maintaining a viable trajectory through an incredibly unpredictable, hostile environment.

52
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:47,100
To really grasp how massive this shift is, I want to look at this amazing historical coincidence.

53
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:54,120
Or honestly, maybe it's less of a coincidence and more of a hidden truth that's just been buried in the dictionary for thousands of years.

54
00:03:54,200 --> 00:03:56,700
Oh, the etymology thing. Yeah, this is wild.

55
00:03:56,800 --> 00:04:00,320
When I was reading through your notes on this, I had to stop and look it up because it blew my mind.

56
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,500
It all comes back to a single ancient Greek word, doesn't it?

57
00:04:03,560 --> 00:04:08,020
It really is one of those linguistic keys that unlocks a whole new way of seeing the world.

58
00:04:08,520 --> 00:04:09,840
The word is kybernetis.

59
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:10,640
Kybernetis.

60
00:04:10,640 --> 00:04:16,820
Right. In ancient Greek, it means the helmsman or the steersman of a ship, the person standing at the back of the boat holding the tiller.

61
00:04:16,920 --> 00:04:18,520
Okay, so the guy steering the boat.

62
00:04:18,980 --> 00:04:29,580
Exactly. And what's so profound here is that this single root birthed two entirely different, seemingly unrelated concepts that we use all the time today.

63
00:04:29,640 --> 00:04:30,560
Okay, lay it on me.

64
00:04:30,980 --> 00:04:37,140
Well, the Romans took kybernetis and formed the Latin word guberner, which literally means to steer or govern.

65
00:04:37,580 --> 00:04:40,140
Wait, so that is where we get the English word government?

66
00:04:40,140 --> 00:04:49,700
Exactly. But then, centuries later, in 1948, the mathematician Norbert Wiener was trying to formalize a new science.

67
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:50,960
A science of what?

68
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:56,000
He was looking at how biological animals and mechanical machines communicate and control themselves.

69
00:04:56,600 --> 00:05:00,040
He needed a word for this new science of feedback loops.

70
00:05:00,040 --> 00:05:02,300
Don't tell me he went back to the Greek word.

71
00:05:02,300 --> 00:05:07,580
He did. He went back to that exact same ancient Greek root and coined the term cybernetics.

72
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:15,080
Wow. So, government and cybernetics are literally linguistic twins. They come from the exact same idea of steering a ship.

73
00:05:15,200 --> 00:05:15,820
They really do.

74
00:05:16,140 --> 00:05:22,360
And if you take a second to think about what a helmsman actually does in the real world, it changes the entire metaphor of power?

75
00:05:22,360 --> 00:05:26,280
Because a helmsman on a wooden ship in the middle of the ocean doesn't command the ocean.

76
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:29,500
Right. They can't stand on the deck and yell at a hurricane to calm down.

77
00:05:29,840 --> 00:05:31,880
I mean, they can try, but it won't work.

78
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:35,920
They can't dictate the ocean currents or pass a law against rogue waves.

79
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:36,680
So, what do they do?

80
00:05:36,820 --> 00:05:42,920
All a helmsman can do is continually estimate the environment around them, predict where the ship is heading,

81
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:48,940
and apply limited, tiny control inputs to the rudder to keep the ship from sinking.

82
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:53,560
That distinction you just made between commanding reality and steering through it,

83
00:05:53,680 --> 00:05:56,600
that seems like the absolute foundation of this entire framework.

84
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:02,020
It really is. Traditional political science has this terrible habit of looking at society

85
00:06:02,020 --> 00:06:04,120
as a state of static equilibrium.

86
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:06,780
Like, if we just write the perfect laws, we're done.

87
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:11,620
Exactly. It assumes that if you just sit down, debate the issues, get the laws right,

88
00:06:11,720 --> 00:06:16,420
and structure the institutions perfectly, you achieve a stable state forever.

89
00:06:16,420 --> 00:06:18,340
It treats society like a photograph.

90
00:06:18,940 --> 00:06:20,800
Once the picture's taken, it's done.

91
00:06:21,200 --> 00:06:25,480
But constitutional cybernetics looks at society as a continuous trajectory.

92
00:06:25,820 --> 00:06:27,960
It's a movie that never stops rolling.

93
00:06:28,120 --> 00:06:29,740
Right. Because things are always changing.

94
00:06:30,040 --> 00:06:34,400
Always. The economy is always mutating. Demographic populations are shifting.

95
00:06:35,020 --> 00:06:36,860
Disruptive technology is advancing.

96
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:38,520
Unpredictable disturbances.

97
00:06:38,740 --> 00:06:44,140
Yes. Like global pandemics, sudden market crashes, or climate disasters.

98
00:06:44,140 --> 00:06:46,900
They're constantly slamming into the hull of the ship.

99
00:06:47,040 --> 00:06:52,600
So if it's a movie, the government is just constantly running what engineers call the classical cybernetic loop.

100
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:56,740
Right. The observe, estimate, compare, control loop.

101
00:06:57,060 --> 00:07:00,200
Observe, estimate, compare, control, and then observe again.

102
00:07:01,400 --> 00:07:02,840
I'm picturing the steersman here.

103
00:07:02,900 --> 00:07:03,100
Yeah.

104
00:07:03,100 --> 00:07:06,700
They feel the spray of the ocean in the direction of the wind.

105
00:07:06,820 --> 00:07:07,760
That's observation, right?

106
00:07:07,780 --> 00:07:08,080
Exactly.

107
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:12,300
Then they take that feeling, and guess how far off course the ship has drifted?

108
00:07:12,540 --> 00:07:13,400
That's estimation.

109
00:07:13,560 --> 00:07:15,040
Yep. They are building a mental model.

110
00:07:15,200 --> 00:07:18,640
Then they look down at the compass to see the heading they're actually supposed to be on.

111
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:19,760
That's comparison.

112
00:07:20,100 --> 00:07:22,400
Then they use their muscles to turn the wooden wheel.

113
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:23,420
That's control.

114
00:07:23,580 --> 00:07:24,840
And then they don't just walk away.

115
00:07:24,840 --> 00:07:29,300
They immediately stand there and feel the wind again to see if that turn actually did anything.

116
00:07:29,440 --> 00:07:30,300
It's an endless loop.

117
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:32,340
That is the exact mechanism.

118
00:07:32,980 --> 00:07:38,720
And Flyxion's argument is that every single thing a government does, no matter how complex or bureaucratic it seems,

119
00:07:39,140 --> 00:07:44,260
is just a specific scaled-up implementation of this exact feedback loop.

120
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:45,040
Wait, really?

121
00:07:45,700 --> 00:07:46,600
Every single thing?

122
00:07:46,780 --> 00:07:48,120
Every single thing.

123
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:52,940
Whether you are looking at a central bank committee adjusting national interest rates to cool down inflation,

124
00:07:53,240 --> 00:07:57,560
or a legislature arguing for three months over an infrastructure budget.

125
00:07:57,740 --> 00:08:01,180
Or a Supreme Court reviewing the constitutionality of a new surveillance law.

126
00:08:01,280 --> 00:08:01,580
Yes.

127
00:08:01,680 --> 00:08:05,600
Or even a local environmental agency tracking pollution in a river.

128
00:08:06,120 --> 00:08:10,760
They are all just executing the observe, estimate, compare, control loop.

129
00:08:10,960 --> 00:08:17,760
So the objective of governance isn't to reach some perfect, static, utopian society where everything is solved forever.

130
00:08:17,760 --> 00:08:18,400
Not at all.

131
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:22,780
The objective is to preserve the system's capacity to keep adapting to the next wave.

132
00:08:23,060 --> 00:08:24,840
Let me see if I can put an analogy on this.

133
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:27,840
So I want to make sure I'm wrapping my head around this shift in values here.

134
00:08:27,940 --> 00:08:28,440
Go for it.

135
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:34,540
It seems to me that traditional politics judges a pilot by how shiny their uniform is,

136
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:36,160
what speeches they give in the terminal,

137
00:08:36,700 --> 00:08:40,500
and maybe by whether the passengers in the cabin voted for them to be in the cockpit.

138
00:08:40,960 --> 00:08:41,320
Sure.

139
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:43,160
That's the classic view of legitimacy.

140
00:08:43,540 --> 00:08:46,480
But cybernetics doesn't care about the uniform or the speeches.

141
00:08:46,480 --> 00:08:51,460
It judges the pilot purely by whether they can keep the plane in the air

142
00:08:51,460 --> 00:08:54,980
when a Category 5 hurricane suddenly forms right in front of them.

143
00:08:55,300 --> 00:08:57,800
That is entirely brutally functional, yes.

144
00:08:57,920 --> 00:09:00,940
I have to push back a little here because I'm feeling a sense of unease.

145
00:09:01,260 --> 00:09:01,580
Uh-oh.

146
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:02,500
What's wrong?

147
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:05,260
Well, isn't this framework a bit, I don't know, cold?

148
00:09:05,260 --> 00:09:08,700
We are talking about human society.

149
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,380
We are talking about laws that put people in prison,

150
00:09:11,840 --> 00:09:14,480
budget debates that determine if children get school lunches,

151
00:09:14,620 --> 00:09:17,040
the messy reality of human lives.

152
00:09:17,180 --> 00:09:17,940
I see where you're going.

153
00:09:17,940 --> 00:09:23,720
And this cybernetic theory seems to reduce all of that passion, morality, and suffering

154
00:09:23,720 --> 00:09:27,160
to an automated thermostat regulating the air conditioning in a house.

155
00:09:27,800 --> 00:09:32,260
It sounds like we are stripping the humanity and the moral weight out of politics entirely.

156
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:34,240
I completely understand that reaction.

157
00:09:34,760 --> 00:09:39,400
It's usually the very first objection people have when you apply control theory to society.

158
00:09:39,660 --> 00:09:41,880
That it feels deeply technocratic and sterile.

159
00:09:41,880 --> 00:09:42,320
Exactly.

160
00:09:42,740 --> 00:09:45,000
But if we dig a little deeper, it's actually the opposite.

161
00:09:45,260 --> 00:09:51,160
It's not about automating humans or reducing us to mindless cogs in a giant machine.

162
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:52,400
Okay, then what is it about?

163
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:59,620
It's about recognizing the severe, unavoidable, and often tragic limitations of human institutions.

164
00:10:00,720 --> 00:10:06,100
A thermostat is a simple controller because it operates in a simple, closed environment.

165
00:10:06,340 --> 00:10:06,520
Right.

166
00:10:06,600 --> 00:10:08,340
A house doesn't change its fundamental nature.

167
00:10:08,580 --> 00:10:09,080
Exactly.

168
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:11,840
But human societies are infinitely complex.

169
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:13,140
They are constantly evolving.

170
00:10:13,740 --> 00:10:19,120
The so-called thermostat of government is constantly being overwhelmed by incomplete information,

171
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:21,260
delayed signals, and human bias.

172
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:22,000
Oh, I see.

173
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:26,160
So acknowledging that government is a cybernetic loop isn't a cold dismissal of humanity.

174
00:10:26,700 --> 00:10:29,380
It's a profound plea for epistemic humility.

175
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:32,200
Meaning we have to admit how little we actually know.

176
00:10:32,540 --> 00:10:32,920
Yes.

177
00:10:32,920 --> 00:10:39,180
It tells us that no leader, no matter how morally righteous their intentions, and no matter how

178
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:44,500
beautiful their speeches are, can successfully govern if they don't have accurate sensors.

179
00:10:44,840 --> 00:10:48,460
Or if the steering wheel they are holding is completely disconnected from the rudder.

180
00:10:48,720 --> 00:10:49,240
Exactly.

181
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:54,400
You can have the most moral pilot in the world, but if their dashboard is telling them they are

182
00:10:54,400 --> 00:11:00,240
going up when they are actually going down, they are going to fly the plane straight into

183
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:00,740
a mountain.

184
00:11:01,100 --> 00:11:03,880
And their good intentions won't save a single passenger.

185
00:11:04,300 --> 00:11:05,460
That's the reality of it.

186
00:11:05,500 --> 00:11:06,420
That makes a lot of sense.

187
00:11:06,740 --> 00:11:11,360
Which leads us to what seems like the most terrifying hurdle the steersman faces.

188
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:17,700
If government is the helmsman and it relies entirely on its instruments to see the ocean,

189
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:20,140
how does it actually see?

190
00:11:20,280 --> 00:11:21,340
Well, this is the kicker.

191
00:11:21,340 --> 00:11:25,520
Because reading through the monograph, the terrifying truth seems to be that it can't.

192
00:11:25,540 --> 00:11:26,520
Not directly, anyway.

193
00:11:26,680 --> 00:11:31,060
You're touching on what the text calls the observer problem, which is perhaps the most

194
00:11:31,060 --> 00:11:33,680
humbling concept in the entire theory.

195
00:11:33,860 --> 00:11:34,760
The observer problem.

196
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:35,720
Unpack that for us.

197
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:40,840
In our daily lives, we casually talk about the state of the economy or the state of public

198
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:42,940
health as if they are physical objects.

199
00:11:43,200 --> 00:11:46,380
Like things you can just put on a scale and weigh or measure with a tape measure.

200
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:46,880
Right.

201
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:50,600
But the true social state is infinite in its dimensionality.

202
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:57,800
It is made up of billions of daily interactions, undocumented emotional states, hidden financial

203
00:11:57,800 --> 00:12:01,420
transactions, microscopic biological realities.

204
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:02,860
So it's just too big to comprehend.

205
00:12:03,120 --> 00:12:03,980
Way too big.

206
00:12:04,040 --> 00:12:11,400
There is no single instrument, no central authority, and no supercomputer on Earth that has direct,

207
00:12:11,860 --> 00:12:15,220
unfettered access to the complete reality of a society.

208
00:12:15,500 --> 00:12:18,100
So the government is essentially always flying blind.

209
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:18,500
Well.

210
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:19,760
It never actually looks out the window.

211
00:12:19,760 --> 00:12:23,980
It's flying strictly on instruments, and the instruments are inherently, unavoidably

212
00:12:23,980 --> 00:12:24,420
flawed.

213
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:25,060
Yikes.

214
00:12:25,400 --> 00:12:30,280
To explain this, the framework leans heavily on the 18th century philosopher Immanuel Kant.

215
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:32,440
Oh, taking us back to philosophy class.

216
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:33,220
Just for a second.

217
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:39,480
Kant made this famous foundational distinction between phenomena, which is reality as we perceive

218
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:42,740
it, filter it, and represent it to ourselves in things in themselves.

219
00:12:42,740 --> 00:12:48,940
The things in themselves being reality as it actually exists in the universe, completely independent

220
00:12:48,940 --> 00:12:50,400
of our ability to observe it.

221
00:12:50,480 --> 00:12:50,960
Exactly.

222
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:57,100
And Flyxion takes Kant's philosophy and applies it directly to political science.

223
00:12:57,460 --> 00:13:00,300
The government never acts on reality in itself.

224
00:13:00,520 --> 00:13:02,500
It is mathematically impossible.

225
00:13:02,500 --> 00:13:04,080
It only ever acts on phenomena.

226
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:04,480
Yes.

227
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:09,860
It acts on an estimated internal state that it has reconstructed from delayed, noisy, and

228
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:11,920
often totally contradictory observations.

229
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:17,360
I want to make sure we don't lose anyone here, because there is a mathematical formula in the text that explains this.

230
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:21,720
And honestly, when I first saw the Greek letters, my eyes totally glazed over.

231
00:13:21,720 --> 00:13:23,220
Yeah, formulas can do that.

232
00:13:23,280 --> 00:13:25,820
But once I sat with it, it became incredibly profound.

233
00:13:26,660 --> 00:13:30,500
The formula is AT, high ET, plus AT.

234
00:13:30,860 --> 00:13:32,860
It looks intimidating, but it's brilliant.

235
00:13:33,160 --> 00:13:34,260
Let's not just read the math.

236
00:13:34,340 --> 00:13:35,200
Let's ground this.

