You just need to build a temporary bridge, a shared estimation space,

487
00:26:09,100 --> 00:26:13,680
where the citizen's lived experience of poverty can be translated into data

488
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:16,160
the economist can actually plug into their model.

489
00:26:16,520 --> 00:26:19,120
And the economist model can be translated into a policy

490
00:26:19,120 --> 00:26:21,300
that actually relieves the citizen's anxiety.

491
00:26:21,700 --> 00:26:22,000
Yes.

492
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:27,400
It's a constant, messy, exhausting, ongoing negotiation of temporary standards.

493
00:26:27,820 --> 00:26:33,920
When that negotiation fails, the distributed estimation network fragments into isolated echo chambers.

494
00:26:34,080 --> 00:26:37,700
And the central government entirely loses the ability to act coherently.

495
00:26:37,840 --> 00:26:38,240
Precisely.

496
00:26:38,240 --> 00:26:43,840
Okay, so before we can steer the ship, we have to agree on a temporary map that we can all read.

497
00:26:44,320 --> 00:26:49,020
But in human society, the government isn't just reading a map that was handed to it by God.

498
00:26:49,420 --> 00:26:52,620
The government is drawing the map while it sails the ship.

499
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:53,060
Right.

500
00:26:53,060 --> 00:26:58,320
Which takes us into how the government decides what is even worth measuring in the first place.

501
00:26:58,320 --> 00:27:01,320
This gets into the deep epistemology of government.

502
00:27:01,820 --> 00:27:04,580
We just talked about how different observers use different maps,

503
00:27:04,820 --> 00:27:09,060
but who decides what goes on the official map that the central controller uses?

504
00:27:09,320 --> 00:27:09,460
Right.

505
00:27:10,040 --> 00:27:12,540
Flyxion points out that the variables a government tracks,

506
00:27:12,660 --> 00:27:16,640
things like unemployment, inflation, mental illness, or poverty,

507
00:27:16,640 --> 00:27:20,460
are not natural facts just waiting out in the wild to be discovered,

508
00:27:20,740 --> 00:27:24,160
like a new species of bird or a rock formation.

509
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:25,980
They are representational constructs.

510
00:27:26,020 --> 00:27:27,440
They are invented categories.

511
00:27:27,600 --> 00:27:30,380
The text cites the friend philosopher Michel Foucault here,

512
00:27:30,760 --> 00:27:32,960
specifically his history of psychiatry.

513
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:34,660
Which I think is a brilliant connection.

514
00:27:35,660 --> 00:27:39,120
Foucault argued that the way we classify madness or mental illness

515
00:27:39,120 --> 00:27:43,140
isn't just a purely objective, neutral scientific discovery.

516
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:47,620
The category of mental illness was created by human institutions.

517
00:27:47,880 --> 00:27:51,220
By the medical establishment, the legal system, public administration.

518
00:27:51,660 --> 00:27:51,860
Right.

519
00:27:52,220 --> 00:27:54,200
And once you invent a category like that,

520
00:27:54,260 --> 00:27:55,940
and once you put it on the official map,

521
00:27:56,300 --> 00:27:59,640
it completely alters the cybernetic control loop of society.

522
00:27:59,880 --> 00:28:02,480
You start building specialized asylums and hospitals.

523
00:28:02,860 --> 00:28:05,020
You pass laws about who can be committed.

524
00:28:05,500 --> 00:28:07,520
You redirect billions of dollars in funding.

525
00:28:07,520 --> 00:28:10,060
The category creates a new reality.

526
00:28:10,380 --> 00:28:12,120
Let's look at an even more universal example.

527
00:28:12,820 --> 00:28:13,300
Unemployment.

528
00:28:13,440 --> 00:28:14,160
That's a great one.

529
00:28:14,260 --> 00:28:16,560
Before the late 19th and early 20th centuries,

530
00:28:17,080 --> 00:28:22,580
the concept of unemployment as a standardized, measurable, statistical category

531
00:28:22,580 --> 00:28:25,220
didn't really exist in the way we think of it today.

532
00:28:25,360 --> 00:28:28,740
People were poor, or they were itinerant, or they were idle,

533
00:28:29,000 --> 00:28:32,940
but the government didn't have a monthly unemployment rate dashboard on CNN.

534
00:28:33,420 --> 00:28:35,960
Because it wasn't formalized as a state variable,

535
00:28:35,960 --> 00:28:37,920
governments didn't try to control it.

536
00:28:38,320 --> 00:28:42,640
If you were out of work, it was a local, moral failing, or just bad luck.

537
00:28:42,640 --> 00:28:45,880
But once the government formalized the category of unemployment,

538
00:28:46,800 --> 00:28:50,440
it fundamentally changed the state space of governance.

539
00:28:50,980 --> 00:28:52,280
It became a target for policy?

540
00:28:52,280 --> 00:28:52,760
Yes.

541
00:28:53,320 --> 00:28:55,840
The government began tracking it, estimating it,

542
00:28:55,960 --> 00:29:00,000
and passing sweeping macroeconomic controls to manipulate it.

543
00:29:00,560 --> 00:29:04,180
The government is continually constructing the coordinate system

544
00:29:04,180 --> 00:29:06,560
through which society becomes observable.

545
00:29:06,780 --> 00:29:09,580
So, we build the map, we invent the categories,

546
00:29:09,780 --> 00:29:11,220
and we gather the messy data.

547
00:29:11,620 --> 00:29:12,440
We turn the wheel.

548
00:29:12,980 --> 00:29:15,940
But how do we know if our steering is actually working?

549
00:29:16,400 --> 00:29:19,900
How do we measure the actual strength or capacity of the government

550
00:29:19,900 --> 00:29:21,140
to act in a crisis?

551
00:29:21,140 --> 00:29:23,800
This is where we get to fiscal reachability.

552
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:24,200
Yes.

553
00:29:24,500 --> 00:29:27,380
This is where I encountered the concept of fiscal reachability,

554
00:29:27,540 --> 00:29:30,220
and it completely blew up my understanding of economics.

555
00:29:30,480 --> 00:29:30,860
I agree.

556
00:29:30,960 --> 00:29:33,040
It is a totally paradigm-shifting concept.

557
00:29:33,340 --> 00:29:36,040
Traditional economics usually evaluates the health of a government

558
00:29:36,040 --> 00:29:38,800
by looking at isolated, instantaneous states.

559
00:29:39,060 --> 00:29:41,300
Like, a pundit will go on television and ask,

560
00:29:41,660 --> 00:29:43,060
what is the current budget deficit?

561
00:29:43,440 --> 00:29:46,380
What is the national debt-to-GDP ratio right this second?

562
00:29:46,680 --> 00:29:48,120
What is the inflation rate this month?

563
00:29:48,120 --> 00:29:49,300
They look at a snapshot.

564
00:29:50,200 --> 00:29:53,180
But control theory tells us that looking at a single point in time

565
00:29:53,180 --> 00:29:57,200
is essentially useless for understanding the resilience of a dynamic system.

566
00:29:57,200 --> 00:29:58,740
So, what should we be looking at?

567
00:29:59,140 --> 00:30:02,340
You have to look at the volume of future states a government can reach

568
00:30:02,340 --> 00:30:04,480
without exhausting its control authority.

569
00:30:05,100 --> 00:30:08,900
That mathematical volume of possibilities is fiscal reachability.

570
00:30:08,900 --> 00:30:10,900
Let me bring back the airplane analogy,

571
00:30:11,100 --> 00:30:14,100
because when I mapped fiscal reachability onto aviation,

572
00:30:14,720 --> 00:30:16,240
it instantly clicked for me.

573
00:30:16,360 --> 00:30:16,980
Let's hear it.

574
00:30:17,360 --> 00:30:22,320
Imagine two passenger airplanes flying side-by-side at 1,000 feet,

575
00:30:22,880 --> 00:30:25,740
both traveling at exactly 500 miles an hour.

576
00:30:26,460 --> 00:30:28,940
If you just look at their isolated state,

577
00:30:29,420 --> 00:30:30,780
their altitude, and their speed,

578
00:30:31,220 --> 00:30:32,000
they look identical.

579
00:30:32,220 --> 00:30:33,280
They're perfectly matched.

580
00:30:33,340 --> 00:30:33,580
Right.

581
00:30:33,580 --> 00:30:36,920
This is like looking at two nations that both have a GDP of $2 trillion

582
00:30:36,920 --> 00:30:39,720
and a debt-to-GDP ratio of 80%.

583
00:30:39,720 --> 00:30:41,280
On paper, they are identical.

584
00:30:41,420 --> 00:30:41,760
Exactly.

585
00:30:42,200 --> 00:30:43,600
But, if you look closer,

586
00:30:44,140 --> 00:30:44,820
under the hood,

587
00:30:45,240 --> 00:30:47,400
plane A has completely full fuel tanks,

588
00:30:47,760 --> 00:30:48,780
a well-rested crew,

589
00:30:49,260 --> 00:30:50,600
fully functioning hydraulics,

590
00:30:50,760 --> 00:30:52,420
and strong structural integrity.

591
00:30:52,800 --> 00:30:53,620
Plane B, however,

592
00:30:53,820 --> 00:30:54,980
is running on fumes,

593
00:30:55,440 --> 00:30:56,560
the flaps are jammed,

594
00:30:56,800 --> 00:30:58,260
the co-pilot is exhausted,

595
00:30:58,260 --> 00:31:01,440
and there's a slow-burning electrical fire in the cargo hold.

596
00:31:01,680 --> 00:31:02,020
Yikes.

597
00:31:02,180 --> 00:31:05,640
Plane B might be cruising comfortably at 30,000 feet right now,

598
00:31:05,760 --> 00:31:11,040
but its reachable set of future trajectories is basically just straight down.

599
00:31:11,400 --> 00:31:13,720
Plane A has a massive reachable set.

600
00:31:13,860 --> 00:31:17,060
It can decide to fly to Paris or Tokyo or New York.

601
00:31:17,180 --> 00:31:18,940
It can climb to avoid a storm.

602
00:31:19,120 --> 00:31:21,740
While plane B has exhausted its control authority,

603
00:31:22,100 --> 00:31:24,060
it is fundamentally crashing,

604
00:31:24,360 --> 00:31:26,220
even though the altitude gauge hasn't moved yet.

605
00:31:26,400 --> 00:31:28,000
That is precisely the difference.

606
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:30,920
Plane B is experiencing actuator saturation.

607
00:31:31,260 --> 00:31:33,780
Its steering wheel no longer controls the rudder.

608
00:31:34,460 --> 00:31:35,940
So, when we evaluate a government,

609
00:31:36,080 --> 00:31:37,760
we shouldn't just look at a spreadsheet and ask,

610
00:31:37,860 --> 00:31:39,020
is the budget balanced today?

611
00:31:39,100 --> 00:31:39,900
We should be asking,

612
00:31:40,420 --> 00:31:42,380
how many admissible future trajectories

613
00:31:42,380 --> 00:31:44,440
does this government still have access to?

614
00:31:44,760 --> 00:31:47,240
Basically, what are our options if things go wrong?

615
00:31:47,360 --> 00:31:47,740
Exactly.

616
00:31:47,920 --> 00:31:50,220
If a massive, unpredictable crisis hits tomorrow,

617
00:31:50,560 --> 00:31:52,440
a new pandemic, a global war,

618
00:31:52,860 --> 00:31:54,820
a catastrophic financial collapse,

619
00:31:55,340 --> 00:31:57,740
does this government have the administrative capacity

620
00:31:57,740 --> 00:31:58,500
to respond?

621
00:31:58,500 --> 00:32:01,220
Does it have the public trust required

622
00:32:01,220 --> 00:32:03,520
to ask citizens to make sacrifices?

623
00:32:03,520 --> 00:32:06,800
Does it have the borrowing power in international markets?

624
00:32:07,120 --> 00:32:09,920
Does it have the legal flexibility to maneuver?

625
00:32:10,600 --> 00:32:11,800
Or is it like Plane B,

626
00:32:12,100 --> 00:32:15,100
where every resource is already tapped out

627
00:32:15,100 --> 00:32:17,760
just to maintain the illusion of level flight?

628
00:32:17,760 --> 00:32:20,380
This totally flips the traditional political debate

629
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:22,640
on austerity versus government spending.

630
00:32:23,140 --> 00:32:25,360
I feel like we've been having the wrong argument for decades.

631
00:32:25,540 --> 00:32:26,220
We really have.

632
00:32:26,580 --> 00:32:27,860
Austerity cutting budgets,

633
00:32:28,340 --> 00:32:30,340
slashing services to balance the books,

634
00:32:30,540 --> 00:32:33,020
is usually framed in political speeches

635
00:32:33,020 --> 00:32:34,540
as a moral virtue.

636
00:32:35,020 --> 00:32:37,540
It's talked about like a household tightening its belt

637
00:32:37,540 --> 00:32:38,360
to be responsible.

638
00:32:38,600 --> 00:32:39,300
And on the flip side,

639
00:32:39,460 --> 00:32:40,680
strategic government borrowing

640
00:32:40,680 --> 00:32:42,500
is framed as an economic stimulus,

641
00:32:42,840 --> 00:32:44,200
putting money in people's pockets.

642
00:32:44,200 --> 00:32:46,740
But under the lens of constitutional cybernetics,

643
00:32:47,080 --> 00:32:48,680
neither of these are moral choices.

644
00:32:49,440 --> 00:32:51,320
They are purely mathematical maneuvers

645
00:32:51,320 --> 00:32:53,440
to either expand or preserve

646
00:32:53,440 --> 00:32:55,280
future admissible trajectories.

647
00:32:55,400 --> 00:32:56,020
Yes, exactly.

648
00:32:56,060 --> 00:32:58,620
If a government enforces brutal austerity,

649
00:32:58,980 --> 00:33:00,080
and in the process,

650
00:33:00,340 --> 00:33:02,000
they destroy their physical infrastructure,

651
00:33:02,560 --> 00:33:03,800
let their bridges collapse,

652
00:33:04,180 --> 00:33:05,660
and gut their education system,

653
00:33:05,960 --> 00:33:07,500
they are drastically shrinking

654
00:33:07,500 --> 00:33:09,140
their future reachable states.

655
00:33:09,440 --> 00:33:11,400
Because a population that isn't educated

656
00:33:11,400 --> 00:33:13,460
can't pivot to a new tech economy.

657
00:33:13,940 --> 00:33:16,040
They might have a beautifully balanced budget

658
00:33:16,040 --> 00:33:17,080
and zero debt,

659
00:33:17,180 --> 00:33:19,120
but their future options are gone.

660
00:33:19,500 --> 00:33:20,060
Cardversely,

661
00:33:20,400 --> 00:33:22,620
if a government borrows a massive amount of money

662
00:33:22,620 --> 00:33:23,760
to build resilient,

663
00:33:24,240 --> 00:33:25,640
next-generation infrastructure

664
00:33:25,640 --> 00:33:26,980
and green energy grids,

665
00:33:27,220 --> 00:33:28,560
they might look irresponsible

666
00:33:28,560 --> 00:33:29,860
on today's balance sheet.

667
00:33:29,960 --> 00:33:31,560
But they are massively expanding

668
00:33:31,560 --> 00:33:33,140
their future maneuverability

669
00:33:33,140 --> 00:33:34,600
when climate shocks hit.

670
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:35,860
You've perfectly captured

671
00:33:35,860 --> 00:33:37,820
the cybernetic view of fiscal policy.

672
00:33:38,320 --> 00:33:39,260
A government collapses

673
00:33:39,260 --> 00:33:41,440
when its reachable set contracts to zero.

674
00:33:42,000 --> 00:33:43,540
It simply runs out of options.

675
00:33:43,860 --> 00:33:44,420
It doesn't matter

676
00:33:44,420 --> 00:33:46,120
if its current economic indicators

677
00:33:46,120 --> 00:33:48,080
look fine to the pundits on TV.

678
00:33:48,680 --> 00:33:50,240
If every resource is tapped out,

679
00:33:50,340 --> 00:33:52,260
if public trust is completely eroded,

680
00:33:52,660 --> 00:33:54,380
and if institutional capability

681
00:33:54,380 --> 00:33:55,380
has rusted away,

682
00:33:55,740 --> 00:33:57,460
the slightest external disturbance

683
00:33:57,460 --> 00:33:58,460
will push the system

684
00:33:58,460 --> 00:34:00,140
outside the admissible manifold

685
00:34:00,140 --> 00:34:01,560
and the society will fracture.

686
00:34:01,900 --> 00:34:03,520
Which brings us to the ultimate

687
00:34:03,520 --> 00:34:05,220
structural backstop

688
00:34:05,220 --> 00:34:06,540
of this entire system.

689
00:34:06,540 --> 00:34:08,960
How do we prevent the helmsman

690
00:34:08,960 --> 00:34:11,120
from intentionally or accidentally

691
00:34:11,120 --> 00:34:13,040
driving us into a state

692
00:34:13,040 --> 00:34:14,400
of zero options?

693
00:34:14,700 --> 00:34:14,820
Right.

694
00:34:15,080 --> 00:34:15,940
Because if the government

695
00:34:15,940 --> 00:34:17,100
is this massive,

696
00:34:17,600 --> 00:34:18,940
powerful control loop,

697
00:34:19,560 --> 00:34:20,760
constantly steering,

698
00:34:20,980 --> 00:34:22,680
reallocating billions of dollars

699
00:34:22,680 --> 00:34:24,640
and constructing the map of reality,

700
00:34:25,100 --> 00:34:27,540
what stops it from overcorrecting?

701
00:34:27,880 --> 00:34:29,540
What stops a panicked helmsman

702
00:34:29,540 --> 00:34:30,840
from spinning the wheel

703
00:34:30,840 --> 00:34:32,340
so hard and so fast

704
00:34:32,340 --> 00:34:33,460
that they rip the rudder

705
00:34:33,460 --> 00:34:34,220
right off the ship

706
00:34:34,220 --> 00:34:35,180
and drown everyone?

707
00:34:35,180 --> 00:34:36,660
In physical control engineering,

708
00:34:36,880 --> 00:34:38,040
every system has strict,

709
00:34:38,240 --> 00:34:39,760
unforgiving operational limits.

710
00:34:40,360 --> 00:34:41,260
An airplane has

711
00:34:41,260 --> 00:34:42,220
structural load limits.

712
00:34:42,600 --> 00:34:43,780
You can pull back on the yoke

713
00:34:43,780 --> 00:34:44,600
as hard as you want,

714
00:34:44,680 --> 00:34:46,100
but if you pull too many Gs,

715
00:34:46,220 --> 00:34:47,040
the physical wings

716
00:34:47,040 --> 00:34:49,140
will simply snap off the fuselage.

717
00:34:49,180 --> 00:34:50,060
And a nuclear reactor

718
00:34:50,060 --> 00:34:51,080
has thermal limits.

719
00:34:51,300 --> 00:34:52,160
Push it too hot,

720
00:34:52,740 --> 00:34:53,720
and it melts down.

721
00:34:53,900 --> 00:34:54,860
These limits aren't goals

722
00:34:54,860 --> 00:34:56,080
that the system is trying to achieve.

723
00:34:56,200 --> 00:34:57,800
They are invariant constraints.

724
00:34:58,340 --> 00:34:59,180
You can fly the plane

725
00:34:59,180 --> 00:35:00,160
anywhere you want

726
00:35:00,160 --> 00:35:01,300
as long as you don't exceed

727
00:35:01,300 --> 00:35:02,200
the structural limits

728
00:35:02,200 --> 00:35:02,900
of the airframe.

729
00:35:02,900 --> 00:35:04,380
Flyxion proposes

730
00:35:04,380 --> 00:35:05,600
a radical idea here.

731
00:35:05,760 --> 00:35:07,200
A constitution is the exact

732
00:35:07,200 --> 00:35:08,480
same mathematical object

733
00:35:08,480 --> 00:35:09,640
for a human society.

734
00:35:09,640 --> 00:35:11,740
So a constitution isn't just

735
00:35:11,740 --> 00:35:13,820
a sacred historical document

736
00:35:13,820 --> 00:35:14,800
written on parchment

737
00:35:14,800 --> 00:35:15,660
by our ancestors.

738
00:35:15,880 --> 00:35:17,420
It's not just a social contract.

739
00:35:17,860 --> 00:35:18,520
In this theory,

740
00:35:18,660 --> 00:35:20,240
it's an invariant manifold.

741
00:35:20,680 --> 00:35:21,740
Mathematically defining

742
00:35:21,740 --> 00:35:23,500
the absolute structural limits

743
00:35:23,500 --> 00:35:25,160
within which all control laws

744
00:35:25,160 --> 00:35:26,680
must operate

745
00:35:26,680 --> 00:35:27,720
to prevent the system

746
00:35:27,720 --> 00:35:28,800
from destroying itself.

747
00:35:28,940 --> 00:35:29,320
Correct.

748
00:35:29,320 --> 00:35:31,580
Ordinary, everyday laws

749
00:35:31,580 --> 00:35:32,880
like tax codes,

750
00:35:33,020 --> 00:35:33,640
speed limits,

751
00:35:33,740 --> 00:35:35,040
or environmental regulations

752
00:35:35,040 --> 00:35:36,560
are just localized

753
00:35:36,560 --> 00:35:37,620
control actions

754
00:35:37,620 --> 00:35:38,320
taking place

755
00:35:38,320 --> 00:35:39,380
inside the allowed

756
00:35:39,380 --> 00:35:40,320
safe space.

757
00:35:40,320 --> 00:35:41,540
But the constitution

758
00:35:41,540 --> 00:35:43,240
defines the rigid boundaries

759
00:35:43,240 --> 00:35:44,640
of the space itself.

760
00:35:44,760 --> 00:35:45,040
It says,

761
00:35:45,520 --> 00:35:47,160
you cannot steer past this point

762
00:35:47,160 --> 00:35:48,140
or the wings come off.

763
00:35:48,200 --> 00:35:48,700
Exactly.

764
00:35:49,100 --> 00:35:50,200
And this leads to

